const cleanCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Profile before you fix', note: 'Count missing values per column, duplicates, impossible ranges (age = 250), type mismatches. You cannot fix what you haven\'t measured — and profiling often reveals the dataset\'s real story first.' },
    { math: '2 · Missingness has a mechanism', note: 'Missing completely at random is safe to drop. Missing NOT at random (survey "income" skipped by the highest earners) means dropping rows biases everything. Ask WHY data is missing before choosing.' },
    { math: '3 · Duplicates and identity', note: 'Two rows with the same customer ≠ duplicate (two orders). Define the key that makes a row unique — and document the decision.' },
    { math: '4 · Validate like a paranoid engineer', note: 'Write assertions into the pipeline: expected ranges, referential integrity, row-count drift. Data silently rots upstream; tests are the smoke alarm.' },
  ],
};

const cleanQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'Survey respondents with the highest incomes skip the income question. Dropping those rows is wrong because the missingness is…', choices: ['completely at random', 'not at random — related to the value itself', 'a duplicate problem', 'an encoding problem'], answer: 1, why: 'When missingness relates to the unobserved value, the remaining rows are a biased sample — the average of what survives is not the average of what is true.' },
    { q: 'Filling missing incomes with the column mean does what to the variance?', choices: ['raises it', 'shrinks it — and invents a cluster at the mean', 'nothing', 'makes it negative'], answer: 1, why: 'Mean imputation injects many identical values, deflating spread and manufacturing a pattern at the mean that was never observed. Flags or model-based imputation with sensitivity checks are the honest options.' },
    { q: 'After a join, your row count jumped from 1 million to 1.8 million. The most likely explanation is…', choices: ['data grew', 'a many-to-many join duplicated rows', 'the database cached results', 'NULLs were filled'], answer: 1, why: 'Joining on non-unique keys fans rows out silently — and every downstream average then double-counts. Row counts before and after every join are the tripwire.' },
    { q: 'Tidy data (Wickham) means…', choices: ['sorted rows', 'one observation per row, one variable per column, one table per kind of observation', 'no missing values', 'CSV format'], answer: 1, why: 'Tidy structure makes every later operation (filter, group, join, model) the same simple move — which is why reshaping into tidy form is the first technical step of most analyses.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/cleaning-messy-data">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data cleaning is <strong>80% of the work and 100% of the reputation risk</strong> —
    the step where silent errors become confident wrong conclusions. Every downstream
    technique in this course (EDA, models, dashboards) assumes the data means what it
    says; cleaning is the process of earning that assumption. The discipline is
    threefold: understand how the data was <em>produced</em> (every column is the output
    of some process with failure modes), decide what each anomaly <em>means</em> (a
    judgment call, made explicit), and encode those decisions as <strong>repeatable
    code rather than one-off spreadsheet edits</strong> — because a cleaning step you
    cannot rerun is a cleaning step you cannot trust or defend.
  </p>
  <p>
    The framing that makes cleaning tractable: messy data is not random damage, it is
    <em>archaeology</em>. Each oddity — the negative ages, the duplicate customers, the
    "N/A" spelled five ways — is a fossil of how the data was captured, merged or
    typed by a human somewhere. Cleaning is reading those fossils correctly, not
    scrubbing them away.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the cleaning workflow — profiling, mechanisms, identity, validation" widget="equationStep" config={cleanCfg} />

  <h2>Where mess comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Knowing the sources tells you where to look. <strong>Manual entry</strong> produces
    transpositions (31 typed as 13), unit slips, and the free-text disease of
    uncontrolled vocabularies ("NY", "New York", "new york city" — three cities or
    one?). <strong>Sensors and logs</strong> produce gaps (the device was offline),
    spikes (the moment of interference), and drift (the sensor slowly miscalibrating).
    <strong>Merging systems</strong> produces identity problems (the same person keyed
    "J Smith" in one database and "Smith, John" in another) and join problems (the
    schemas disagree on what a "user" is). <strong>Exports and handoffs</strong> produce
    encoding rot (UTF-8 read as Latin-1 turns "café" into "cafÃ©") and truncation (a
    column sized for 40 characters quietly amputating surnames). Each source has
    characteristic failures — which is why the first question of cleaning is never
    "what is wrong?" but "where did this come from?"
  </p>

  <h2>Profile before you fix</h2>
  <p>
    Profiling is the systematic first look, and it is cheap: for every column, count
    missing values, count distinct values, look at min/max, sample the weird rows. A
    worked profile on a 10,000-row customer table: age has minimum −3 and maximum 250
    (two impossible-value families — likely a sign error and a birthdate subtraction
    bug); email is missing in 2% of rows and duplicated in 4% (people with two
    accounts); signup_date contains values in 2075 (a timezone or epoch bug);
    country has 212 distinct values where there are ~200 countries (typos and
    variants). Ten minutes of profiling just produced a complete work plan — and
    notice the order of operations: <strong>profile first, hypothesise second, fix
    third, document always</strong>. Fixing before profiling means fixing your
    guesses.
  </p>

  <h2>Missingness: the mechanism is the decision</h2>
  <p>
    Missing data is not one problem but three, and the distinction decides everything.
    <strong>Missing completely at random</strong> (a sensor dropped 2% of packets) —
    dropping those rows is safe; you lose a little precision and no truth.
    <strong>Missing at random given other variables</strong> (younger users skip the
    phone field, and you know age) — dropping biases by group, but the pattern is
    recoverable from the other columns. <strong>Missing not at random</strong> — the
    missingness relates to the unobserved value itself: the highest earners skip the
    income question, the sickest patients skip the follow-up visit. Here dropping rows
    biases every statistic silently, and even imputation needs explicit modelling of
    the missingness. The practical protocol: measure the missingness rate per column,
    test whether it differs across groups, and write down the suspected mechanism
    before choosing a treatment. Options in rough order of honesty: keep missing as an
    explicit category or flag (often best); impute with median for skewed data (mean
    for clean symmetric data) with a sensitivity check; impute by model (predict from
    the other columns); drop rows (only when missingness is provably random and rare).
    Mean-imputation's specific sin: it injects a fake cluster at the mean and shrinks
    the variance — the data after imputation literally looks more average than the
    world was.
  </p>

  <h2>Identity, duplicates and keys</h2>
  <p>
    "Duplicate" is meaningless until you define what makes two rows the same thing.
    Two rows with the same customer ID are not duplicates if each is an order; two
    rows with the same order ID probably are (a re-run of the export? a double
    submission?). The unit of analysis defines the key: one row per order, per
    customer, per customer-month? — three different datasets, three different answers
    to "is this a duplicate?". Then the harder layer: <strong>entity resolution</strong>,
    the same real-world entity under different keys ("J. Smith" and "Smith, John" and
    "john.smith@…"). Exact matching fails; fuzzy matching helps but makes judgment
    calls (two John Smiths are two people); and the decisions must be documented,
    because they change every count downstream. The discipline: define the grain and
    the key in writing at the top of the analysis, deduplicate with respect to that
    key, and report how many rows each decision removed.
  </p>

  <h2>Outliers and impossible values</h2>
  <p>
    An outlier is only "bad data" sometimes, and the diagnosis differs by case. A
    temperature of −273.15 °C is impossible (a constant was subtracted twice — a bug
    to fix). An age of 250 is impossible (a birthdate subtraction bug, or 25 with a
    fat-fingered zero). A transaction of 4,999,999 might be a data-entry slip — or a
    whale customer, the most important row in the dataset. The distinction that
    matters: <strong>impossible</strong> values (outside physical or definitional
    bounds) are errors to correct or remove; <strong>implausible</strong> values are
    findings to investigate before touching. The cost of guessing wrong is legendary:
    NASA lost the Mars Climate Orbiter in 1999 because one team's software reported
    thrust in pound-force seconds while the navigation software expected
    newton-seconds — a unit mismatch worth a 125-million-dollar spacecraft, and the
    standing proof that "obviously wrong" values deserve a root-cause question
    ("why does this column contain this?") rather than a delete key.
  </p>

  <h2>Joins, merges and the shape of the data</h2>
  <p>
    Cleaning is not only per-column; the <em>shape</em> of the table is a quality
    decision. Hadley Wickham's <strong>tidy data</strong> standard (2014) — one
    observation per row, one variable per column, one kind of observation per table
    — is the target shape, because every later operation (filter, group, join,
    model) becomes the same simple move on tidy data. Real exports violate it
    constantly: years spread across columns (1912, 1913, 1914 as headers), values
    stored in headers, multiple observations crammed into one cell ("A, B, C"). The
    fixes are mechanical (melt/pivot), but do them <em>first</em> — reshaping after
    analysis means redoing the analysis. And the join itself is a cleaning hazard:
    a many-to-many merge (joining orders to items where both sides have repeats)
    fans rows out silently — the row count is the tripwire, checked before and
    after every join, every time.
  </p>

  <h2>Types, encodings, dates: the boringest errors</h2>
  <p>
    The retraction-grade errors are usually unglamorous. <strong>Types</strong>:
    "1,234" read as text; ZIP codes as numbers (losing leading zeros); IDs as floats
    (15 digits, then silently rounded — spreadsheet precision rot). <strong>Encodings</strong>:
    UTF-8 read as Latin-1 garbles every accented character; the fix is trivial once
    diagnosed and invisible until it is. <strong>Dates and timezones</strong>: the
    deepest pit in applied work. "2024-03-01" in UTC versus local time shifts
    daily aggregates; Excel's date serial numbers and 1-based centuries create
    phantom decades; a naive datetime crossing a daylight-saving boundary duplicates
    or deletes an hour of data. The defences are habits, not cleverness: parse dates
    explicitly with a stated timezone; keep one canonical timezone internally; store
    types in a schema, not in hope; and read a sample of raw bytes when text looks
    wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Validation: tests as the smoke alarm</h2>
  <p>
    Clean is not a state, it is a property you assert and re-assert. Data rots
    upstream: a schema changes on the source system, a partner starts sending
    negative quantities, a "temporary" CSV replacement ships on a Friday. The
    engineering answer is validation in the pipeline — assertions that run on every
    load: row counts within an expected band; no nulls in key columns; values within
    physical ranges; referential integrity (every order references an existing
    customer); freshness (today's file exists). Frameworks like Great Expectations
    package these as declarative checks, but the idea matters more than the tool:
    <strong>every cleaning decision becomes a test</strong>, so the day the data
    changes underneath you, something screams instead of a dashboard quietly
    turning wrong. The final discipline is provenance: a cleaning script (with its
    decisions documented inline) is evidence; a hand-edited spreadsheet is a
    confession waiting to be discovered.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked cleaning session: one raw CSV</h2>
  <p>
    The discipline at working scale. Input: sales.csv, 50,000 rows, 12 columns,
    exported by a partner. <strong>Profile</strong>: quantity has negatives (returns?
    errors?); price has a text column masquerading as numeric ("1,234.50" with
    thousands separators); order_date spans 1970–2075 (epoch bugs at both ends —
    empty dates coerced to zero and a timezone shifted a century); customer_id is
    3% duplicated; region has 47 values for 8 real regions. <strong>Decide and
    document</strong>: negatives are returns — keep them, flag them (they are real
    transactions, and net revenue needs them); prices parsed by stripping
    separators, errors logged; dates before 2000 or after next year set to null and
    counted (they are junk, but counting junk is monitoring); region values mapped
    through a published lookup table, unmapped values output to a review file —
    never silently dropped. <strong>Encode</strong>: every decision becomes a line
    in a script with a comment naming the reason; a validation block asserts the
    final row count is within 5% of input, no price is negative, and the region
    mapping covers at least 99% of rows. <strong>Report</strong>: the summary
    numbers — rows in, rows out, dropped by rule, imputed, flagged — travel with
    the cleaned file. Forty minutes, one repeatable artefact, and a dataset that
    can be defended line by line.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four expert notes. <strong>Probabilistic record linkage</strong>: the formal
    solution to entity resolution — Fellegi–Sunter-style scoring weighs agreement and
    disagreement on name, address, birthdate to estimate match probability, with
    thresholds trading false merges against false splits; both error types have
    asymmetric costs, and the threshold is a business decision. <strong>Sensitivity
    analysis as the professional standard</strong>: for every judgment call
    (dropped rows, imputed values, deduplicated entities), rerun the analysis under
    the alternative choice; if conclusions flip, the conclusion was never about the
    data. <strong>Data versioning and lineage</strong>: datasets that change
    underneath analyses are the reproducibility crisis's industrial wing — hash your
    inputs, log the cleaning commit, and be able to answer "which version of this
    table produced this figure?". And <strong>the ethics of the delete key</strong>:
    removing rows changes the population your results describe — a cleaned dataset
    is a series of claims about who was included and excluded, and those claims
    deserve the same scrutiny as the findings.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Wickham, "Tidy Data", Journal of Statistical Software (2014); Kimball, The Data Warehouse Toolkit; Great Expectations data-quality documentation; NASA, Mars Climate Orbiter Mishap Investigation Board report (1999).</p>
